A||N||3||Roy Grooms||poly or rubber bushings That is the Question ?||||1||
Z||000000||Roy Grooms||09-22-2001||01:26 PM|||| I get ready to replace upper control and cradle bushings, I read good and bad about poly,  I put the poly sway link in and had to take them out, (I drove Tanks that rode better) and put new ruber ones in ,OH much better, Its a 84 2m4 , now what do i do for the upper and cradle, leaning toward the rubber ones<BR>             Roystoy<BR>             84 2m4||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||09-22-2001||01:48 PM||||Poly bushings are great if you want a stiff ride. If you want the normal ride quality stick with rubber. On the Dukes the dogbone on top will cause more engine vibration to be felt if it is poly. The V-6's are not that noticable. Control arm bushings being poly will actually make the steering easier due to less flex in the control arm but you will feel the bumps in the road a little more. Changing the cradle to poly and leaving the engine mounts rubber is a good thing. Ride quality doesn't change but cornering will feel better since the cradle won't flex much. Accelleration will feel better due to less flex also. <BR>My suggestion if you want to keep the stock ride quality keep the control arm bushings rubber but change the cradle bushings to poly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-22-2001||03:30 PM||||Polly cradle mounts are cheap and nearly eliminate cradle movement. This keeps the alignment more constant. Polly or if you can afford them solid cradle mounts are always a good idea. Since the cradle doesn't move you don't have to worry about the bushings squeaking.<P>Myself... I won't use polly in the front control arms. I might use it in the rear if I had a big motor to reduce rear arm movement caused by torque loading.<P>Fresh rubber bushings are allot tighter tham most people think. The problem is most people compare polly to the old spent rubber that was in there. Compared to new rubber polly isn't that much better.<P>Rubber bushings do have some installation rules. I think they are in one of my cave articles.... If you don't follow the rules then you will destroy them almost as soon as you let the car off the jacks. The rule is simple... don't thighten thru bolts until you put the car's weight on the suspension. This way the bushings don't get twisted out of spec.<P>Something that allot of people don't understand is that polly is a bearing function as used in control arms. It provides the same friction over it's entire rotation.<P>Rubber is a torsional spring. It actully tightens as the suspension moves. (This is why it's important to install them correctly...) The rubber bushing is considerably tighter at the ends of travel than at rest. This effect slightly stiffens the total spring rate of the suspension.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Roy Grooms||09-22-2001||09:59 PM|||| Thanks, you both made my mine up, just what I was thinking,I well go with rubber on the arms and poly on the cradle. the cradle ,I feel are bad too. ,ya I get a waggle out of the back alot. Thanks again<BR>        Roystoy<BR>        84 2m4||||reg||1
